{
 "id": "2019-meidenbauer-do",
 "name": "Do Children Prefer Nature? Picture Sorting",
 "description": "Rebuilt from the published description of Meidenbauer, Stenfors, Young, Layden, Schertz, Kardan, Decety & Berman (2019) and the task paper by Meidenbauer, Stenfors, Ingram & Berman (2019, MethodsX). Children aged 4–11 and their parents sort sets of four nature and city pictures by dragging them from the one they like least (frowny face) to the one they like most (smiley face). There are 10 sets drawn from one picture set of 10 (5 nature, 5 urban); every picture appears four times and every pair of pictures meets at least once, as in the paper's app. Picture and start positions are random. The researcher enters role (child or parent), age and gender; a family code pairs each child with their parent. In the paper, parents preferred nature, while children preferred the urban scenes, and that urban preference weakened with age. Differences from the original study: (1) The paper used an Android tablet app; this is a web page that works on any tablet, with drag or tap-to-swap. (2) The paper's two 10-picture sets were matched on adult preference (from the authors' validation ratings) and are on OSF (osf.io/xj3pk). The bundled sets are 2 × (5 nature + 5 urban) pictures from the same CC BY source set (Berman et al., 2014), chosen at random and not matched on preference. For a faithful replication, replace folders set1 and set2 with the OSF sets. (3) The practice round with pictures of children's bedrooms is replaced by spoken instructions on screen. (4) The family code is an addition.",
